import System.IO.Console;
program AgentsExe;

// The stretches of a frame the meter measures, one constant per stretch.
//
// THE SIMULATION PHASES ARE GONE WITH THE SIMULATION. What was here -- Census, Regrow, Breeding,
// Beasts, Prowl, Living, Thinking, Moving -- named the passes of an agent world that has been
// deleted to be rebuilt. The METER itself stays, because it is not an agent thing: it is how the
// renderer knows which of its own passes costs the frame, and that question survives the rewrite
// unchanged. Whatever the new simulation calls its passes gets added here.
//
// EVERY STRETCH OF THE LOOP BELONGS TO EXACTLY ONE PHASE, and that is not tidiness. The three
// renderer phases once summed to 2.1 ms while the frame itself took 35.7, so the meter reported
// shares of six per cent of the time and called them the frame. A profile that covers part of a
// loop does not merely omit the rest: it points at the WRONG thing, because the biggest number in it
// looks like the biggest number there is.
//
// The splits below have the same cause. `Moving` held the walk and the town's thinking together and
// read as 97% of the frame while saying nothing about which of the two to attack. `Deciding` said
// 93% and nothing about which of its five passes owned it.
//
// NINE OF THESE WERE MEASURING NOTHING, and they are gone (S0.2). `People`, `Fauna`, `Town`,
// `Borders`, `Teaching`, `Ordering`, `Sending`, `Building` and `Settling` were passes of the
// deleted simulation that outlived it: no line in the program ever laps them, so the meter carried
// nine counters that could only ever read zero. Each one comes back when its pass does, and by
// then it comes back with its own name -- see below.
//
// THE REPORT'S NAME FOR A PHASE IS THIS IDENTIFIER, lower-cased, and there is no second place to
// write it. It used to be a sixteen-branch `if` cascade in the `Meter` with a fall-through at the
// end, so a constant added here reported under the LAST one's name (ledger S0-2). The failure is
// quiet in the worst way -- the row appears, it carries a name, and the name belongs to another
// pass, so the reader optimizes whatever the report happens to have blamed.
public bundle Agents {
    public namespace Sim {

        // SEALED, so a `match` over a phase must name every one of them. The report already derives
        // its names rather than listing them, which is the same guarantee for the same reason: a
        // phase added later cannot end up unaccounted for in a place that was written before it.
        public sealed enum Phase {
            // the renderer
            Terrain, Fauna, People, Town, Draw, Post,
            // and the rest of the frame
            Input, Tick, Hud, Swap
        }
    }
}
